# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "On the curve you measured, $P_1$ first reaches its maximum at the $\\pi$ pulse ($\\theta = \\pi$). Now suppose a device is miscalibrated, so that a *nominal* angle $\\theta$ on the x-axis actually produces a rotation of $1.05\\,\\theta$.\n"
msgstr "Pada lengkung yang anda ukur, $P_1$ mula-mula mencapai maksimumnya pada denyutan $\\pi$ ($\\theta = \\pi$). Sekarang andaikan sebuah peranti tersalah tentu ukur, supaya sudut *nominal* $\\theta$ pada paksi-x sebenarnya menghasilkan putaran $1.05\\,\\theta$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**(a)** At what nominal angle would the first maximum of $P_1$ now appear?\n"
"**(b)** Would applying a single $\\pi$ pulse be a sensitive way to detect this 5% error?\n"
msgstr ""
"**(a)** Pada sudut nominal berapakah maksimum pertama $P_1$ kini muncul?\n"
"**(b)** Adakah menggunakan satu denyutan $\\pi$ cara yang sensitif untuk mengesan ralat 5% ini?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
msgstr ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Jawapan\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**(a)** The maximum appears where the *actual* rotation equals $\\pi$, that is, at the nominal angle $\\theta = \\pi/1.05 \\approx 0.95\\pi$ &mdash; a small leftward shift of the peak.\n"
msgstr "**(a)** Maksimum muncul apabila putaran *sebenar* sama dengan $\\pi$, iaitu pada sudut nominal $\\theta = \\pi/1.05 \\approx 0.95\\pi$ &mdash; anjakan kecil puncak ke kiri.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-12
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "**(b)** No. A nominal $\\pi$ pulse produces an actual rotation of $1.05\\pi$, giving $P_1 = \\sin^2(1.05\\pi/2) \\approx 0.994$ instead of $1$ &mdash; a change too small to see against measurement noise. This is why, to *calibrate* precisely, NMR spectroscopists amplify the error by applying the rotation many times (for example the $360^\\circ$ method, or a long pulse train), so a small per-pulse error accumulates into a large, measurable deviation. We return to this idea in the challenge problems.\n"
msgstr "**(b)** Tidak. Denyut $\\pi$ nominal menghasilkan putaran sebenar $1.05\\pi$, memberikan $P_1 = \\sin^2(1.05\\pi/2) \\approx 0.994$ dan bukannya $1$ &mdash; perubahan yang terlalu kecil untuk dilihat berbanding hingar pengukuran. Inilah sebabnya, untuk *menentukur* dengan tepat, ahli spektroskopi NMR menguatkan ralat dengan menggunakan putaran itu berkali-kali (contohnya kaedah $360^\\circ$, atau rentetan denyut yang panjang), supaya ralat kecil bagi setiap denyut terkumpul menjadi sisihan besar yang boleh diukur. Kita kembali kepada idea ini dalam masalah cabaran.\n"

